        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: unblock close apply throughput",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n- raise default close-apply limits and checkpoints from 5 to 20\n- prioritize ready close decisions and bounded close-coverage proofs before speculative promotion scans\n- retry malformed successful GitHub JSON responses in core and repair paths\n\n## Why\n\nClose runs were spending their small windows on slow policy-gated or speculative candidates before reaching already-reviewed close decisions. Successful `gh` calls that returned malformed JSON also failed the whole lane instead of retrying. Together these left a large reviewed closure backlog despite stricter close policy.\n\n## Impact\n\nDefault apply runs can drain up to 20 fresh closes per checkpoint, preserve shareable continuation semantics, and reach high-confidence close work first. Eligibility, freshness, protected-label, author, proof, and policy gates are unchanged.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `pnpm run check`\n- manual ClawSweeper cleanup validated the affected backlog classes against live PR heads and canonical replacements\n",
